## 1. What is it?

"DOM fully loaded" means knowing it's safe to touch the page's elements in code. Two events tell you this: `DOMContentLoaded` (the HTML is parsed into the DOM) and `load` (everything — images, stylesheets, iframes — has also finished). `document.readyState` lets you check the current state anytime, even after these events already happened.

## 2. Why introduced?

Old scripts ran inline, in the order they appeared in the HTML. That meant a `<script>` tag could run before an element further down the page even existed — a constant source of "cannot read property of null" bugs when code tried to grab an element too early. Browsers needed clear signals so code could say "run this once the DOM (or the whole page) is actually ready," instead of guessing based on where the script tag sits, or using fragile `setTimeout` hacks.

## 3. How does it work?

`document.readyState` moves through three stages during a page load: `"loading"` (still parsing HTML), `"interactive"` (parsing is done, the DOM is fully built and usable, but images/stylesheets/iframes might still be loading), and `"complete"` (everything, including those subresources, has finished).

`DOMContentLoaded` fires the moment `readyState` hits `"interactive"` — as soon as the DOM is parsed and safe to query, without waiting for images or stylesheets. It does wait for regular `<script>` tags to finish running (the parser pauses for those), but not for async scripts or subresources.

`load` fires on `window` once `readyState` hits `"complete"` — every image, stylesheet, and iframe has finished loading. This always happens at the same time as or after `DOMContentLoaded`, never before.

Both events only fire once. If you attach a listener after the event already happened, it will never fire for you — that's exactly what `readyState` is for: check it first, and only attach a listener if you haven't reached the state you need yet.

```javascript
function onDomReady(callback) {
  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', callback);
  } else {
    // readyState is already 'interactive' or 'complete' — DOMContentLoaded already fired
    callback();
  }
}
```

Modern HTML also gives you `<script defer>`, which waits until parsing finishes before running (right before `DOMContentLoaded`) without blocking the parser, and `<script type="module">`, which defers by default for the same reason. Both mostly replace the old trick of putting your script tag at the bottom of `<body>`.

## 4. Example

```html
<script>
  console.log('readyState at top of <head>:', document.readyState); // "loading"

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    console.log('DOM parsed, safe to query elements:', document.readyState); // "interactive"
    console.log(document.getElementById('main')); // found — DOM exists
  });

  window.addEventListener('load', () => {
    console.log('Everything loaded, including images:', document.readyState); // "complete"
  });
</script>

<body>
  <div id="main">
    <img src="large-photo.jpg">
  </div>
</body>
```

In an Angular app, you rarely need any of this directly. Angular's own bootstrap already runs after the DOM is parsed. For finer control — knowing when a specific component's view is actually rendered — Angular gives you its own hooks: `ngAfterViewInit()`, or in modern Angular, `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()` for code that needs the real browser DOM (like measuring an element or mounting a third-party library).

```typescript
import { Component, afterNextRender, ElementRef, viewChild } from '@angular/core';

@Component({ selector: 'app-chart', template: `<div #chartHost></div>` })
export class ChartComponent {
  chartHost = viewChild.required<ElementRef<HTMLDivElement>>('chartHost');

  constructor() {
    afterNextRender(() => {
      // Guaranteed the view's DOM nodes exist and are attached — safe to measure/mount a chart library here.
      console.log(this.chartHost().nativeElement.getBoundingClientRect());
    });
  }
}
```

## 5. Example Explanation – Execution Flow

At the top of the `<script>` in `<head>`, the parser hasn't reached `<body>` yet, so `readyState` reads `"loading"`. The `DOMContentLoaded` listener is set up before that event can fire, so it runs reliably once parsing finishes — at that point `getElementById('main')` works because the DOM is fully built, even though `large-photo.jpg` might still be downloading. The `load` listener fires later, once the image finishes and `readyState` reaches `"complete"`. In the Angular example, `afterNextRender()` runs its callback only after Angular has rendered the component's view into real, attached DOM nodes — so `chartHost().nativeElement` is guaranteed to exist by then. It's the Angular version of "wait for the DOM," just scoped to one component instead of the whole page.

## 6. When does this show up?

Any time you're working with plain JS or a third-party script that touches the DOM but can't guarantee when it runs relative to the page — widget embeds, analytics snippets, non-Angular libraries dropped into a page. In Angular apps, it comes up specifically when wiring up DOM-measuring or DOM-mutating libraries (charts, maps, rich text editors) that need real, attached DOM nodes — which is why you'd reach for `afterNextRender()` instead of `ngOnInit()` (which can run before the view's DOM is attached) or the plain `DOMContentLoaded`/`load` events (which fire once for the whole page, not per component).

## 7. Advantages/Disadvantages

`DOMContentLoaded` is fast — your code runs as soon as the DOM is safe to touch, without waiting on slow images. The catch: anything depending on image size, iframe content, or other subresources isn't ready yet, so layout-dependent code can break if you use it too early. `load` guarantees everything is ready, but it's slower and can be delayed by large downloads. Checking `readyState` directly is the safest habit, since it works no matter when your script runs — but you have to remember to do it. A plain `addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', cb)` with no `readyState` check silently does nothing if your script runs after the event already fired (for example, a `<script>` tag added dynamically, late).

## 8. Common Interview Traps

Mixing up `DOMContentLoaded` (DOM parsed) with `load` (everything, images included, finished) — a very common mistake. Forgetting that a listener attached after the event already fired simply never runs, and not knowing `readyState` fixes that. Thinking `defer`/`type="module"` scripts run *during* parsing — they're fetched during parsing, but they actually run after parsing finishes, in order, just before `DOMContentLoaded`. In Angular: assuming `ngOnInit()` guarantees the view's DOM is rendered and attached — it doesn't always. `ngAfterViewInit()` or `afterNextRender()` are the right hooks for DOM-dependent work.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

What's the difference in execution order between a `defer` script and an `async` script, relative to `DOMContentLoaded`?

Why might `window.onload` fire much later than expected on a page with lots of images?

In Angular, when would you use `afterNextRender()` vs `afterRender()` vs `ngAfterViewInit()`?

How would you detect "DOM ready" for content added dynamically (via `innerHTML` or a framework), where no native browser event fires at all?

## 10. Quick Answer (say this first)

Use `DOMContentLoaded` when you just need the DOM parsed and queryable, and don't care about images or stylesheets. Use `window.load` when you need everything, subresources included, fully loaded. Always check `document.readyState` first, since both events fire only once, and a listener attached too late never fires. In Angular, the equivalent tool is `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()`, scoped to a single component rather than the whole page.

## 11. Interview-Ready Answer (full)

Detecting a "fully loaded" DOM really means picking the right signal for what you actually need. `document.readyState` moves through `"loading"` → `"interactive"` → `"complete"`. `DOMContentLoaded` fires the moment it hits `"interactive"` — the HTML is fully parsed into a DOM tree, so it's safe to query and change elements, but images, stylesheets, and iframes might still be loading. `window`'s `load` event fires later, once `readyState` reaches `"complete"` — every subresource is done too.

Since both events only fire once, the safe habit is to check `readyState` first: if it's already past `"loading"`, the DOM is ready and you can run your code immediately. Otherwise, attach the listener and wait — because a listener added after the event already fired simply never gets called.

In Angular, you rarely deal with these directly. Angular's bootstrap already runs after the page is parsed. For code that specifically needs real, attached DOM — like measuring an element or mounting a chart or map library — the idiomatic hook is `afterNextRender()` (or `afterRender()` for work that needs to repeat after every render). It guarantees the component's view has actually been rendered into the DOM, scoped to that one component rather than the whole document.

## 12. Quick Revision

`readyState`: `"loading"` → `"interactive"` (DOM parsed, `DOMContentLoaded` fires) → `"complete"` (everything loaded, `load` fires). Both events fire once — check `readyState` before attaching a listener, since late listeners never fire. `defer`/`module` scripts run right before `DOMContentLoaded`, in order, without blocking the parser. Angular equivalent: `afterNextRender()`/`afterRender()` per component, not `ngOnInit()`.

## 13. Memory Trick

**"DOMContentLoaded = the skeleton's built. `load` = it's dressed, fed, and the photos have arrived. Check `readyState` first — you can't RSVP to a party that already happened."**
